from pack_writer import Writer
w=Writer('2025-june','-q001-060')
def add(n,topic,p,o,c,h,s,t,e,track='Statistics'):w.add(n,track,topic,'Moderate',p,o,c,h,s,t,e)
add(51,'Stochastic Processes',r'''Z₁,Z₂,… are iid discrete uniform on {1,2,…,2024}. Put Yₙ=Σᵢ₌₁ⁿZᵢ for n≥2 and let Xₙ be the remainder of Yₙ modulo 2025. Which is true?''',[r'lim P(Xₙ=0)=1/2026.',r'lim P(Xₙ=0)=1/2025.',r'lim P(Xₙ=0)=1/2024.',r'lim P(Xₙ=0)=1/2023.'],'B',['From any residue, the next increment reaches every other residue with equal probability.','Let pₙ=P(Xₙ=0); derive pₙ₊₁=(1−pₙ)/2024.'],r'''A nonzero increment modulo 2025 cannot leave a residue unchanged. From each nonzero current residue, exactly one of the 2024 increments takes it to zero. Hence pₙ₊₁=(1−pₙ)/2024. Its fixed point p satisfies 2024p=1−p, giving p=1/2025. Moreover pₙ₊₁−1/2025=−(pₙ−1/2025)/2024, so the deviations decay geometrically and the limit is 1/2025.''',r'''A random walk modulo an integer m is a finite-state Markov chain whose transition probabilities depend only on the current residue and the increment law. If the increment is uniform on all nonzero residues, a transition is equally likely to any different state and impossible to the same state. The uniform distribution on the m states is stationary because each transition-matrix column sums to one. For a specific target state, its probability satisfies pₙ₊₁=(1−pₙ)/(m−1), with fixed point 1/m. Subtracting the fixed point produces a geometric recurrence with multiplier −1/(m−1), whose magnitude is below one for m>2. This directly proves convergence without a general Markov-chain theorem. Distinguish the number m of possible remainders from the m−1 possible nonzero increments.''',[('Uniformly add 1,2,3,4 modulo 5. Find the limiting probability of residue 0.','The recursion is pₙ₊₁=(1−pₙ)/4, so its fixed point is 1/5 and deviations have factor −1/4. The limit is 1/5.'),('Uniformly add 1 modulo 2 starting at residue 0. Does the zero-state probability converge?','The state alternates deterministically between 0 and 1. Thus pₙ alternates 1 and 0; the recurrence multiplier is −1, so convergence fails.')])
add(52,'Probability Distributions',r'''Brand I battery life has density e^{−x} for x>0. Brand II has density (x/4)e^{−x/2} for x>0. Mobiles use I and II with probabilities 20% and 80%. Find the probability battery life exceeds 2 years.''',[r'(13/5)e^{−2}',r'(1/5)(e^{−2}+2e^{−1})',r'(1/5)(e^{−2}+8e^{−1})',r'(1/5)(4e^{−2}+2e^{−1})'],'C',['Use total probability over the two battery brands.','Integrate the Brand II density from 2 to infinity; integration by parts gives 2e^{−1}.'],r'''For Brand I, survival probability is ∫₂∞e^{−x}dx=e^{−2}. For Brand II, an antiderivative of (x/4)e^{−x/2} is −(x/2+1)e^{−x/2}; its tail at 2 equals 2e^{−1}. Thus P(T>2)=(1/5)e^{−2}+(4/5)(2e^{−1})=(1/5)(e^{−2}+8e^{−1}).''',r'''The survival function of a nonnegative continuous random variable is S(t)=P(T>t)=∫_t^∞f(x)dx. An exponential distribution with rate λ has survival e^{−λt}; a gamma distribution with shape two and scale β has density xe^{−x/β}/β² and survival e^{−t/β}(1+t/β), obtainable by integration by parts. When a population is a mixture of brands or subgroups, the law of total probability weights each conditional survival probability by its subgroup proportion. The mixture distribution is generally not itself an exponential or gamma distribution with averaged parameters. Rates and scales are reciprocals, so identify the density carefully before using a formula. Population proportions apply to the conditional probabilities, not to the time threshold or to the density parameters.''',[('Half a population has Exp(1) life and half Exp(2). Find survival at t=1.','Total probability gives (1/2)e^{−1}+(1/2)e^{−2}; averaging the rates would not give this same mixture.'),('A gamma life has shape 2 and scale 3. Find survival at t=3.','The shape-two formula gives e^{−3/3}(1+3/3)=2e^{−1}.')])
add(53,'Statistical Inference',r'''X has P(X=0)=θ/3, P(X=1)=1−θ/2, P(X=2)=θ/6, θ∈(0,1). A sample of size 90 has counts 20,60,10 for 0,1,2. Find the maximum likelihood estimate of θ.''',[r'1/3',r'1/2',r'2/3',r'3/4'],'C',['Ignoring constants, the likelihood is θ³⁰(1−θ/2)⁶⁰.','Differentiate its logarithm and solve the score equation.'],r'''The likelihood is proportional to (θ/3)²⁰(1−θ/2)⁶⁰(θ/6)¹⁰, hence θ³⁰(1−θ/2)⁶⁰. Its log derivative is 30/θ−30/(1−θ/2). Setting this to zero gives θ=1−θ/2 and θ=2/3. The second derivative −30/θ²−15/(1−θ/2)² is strictly negative, so this interior point is the unique maximum. It lies in (0,1).''',r'''Maximum likelihood estimation chooses parameter values maximizing the probability or density of the observed sample. For iid discrete observations recorded as category counts n_j, the likelihood is proportional to ∏p_j(θ)^{n_j}; the multinomial coefficient is constant in θ and can be dropped. Taking logarithms turns products into sums and preserves maximizers because the logarithm is increasing. An interior candidate solves the score equation, the derivative of the log likelihood. Strict negative second derivative establishes uniqueness of a maximum on an interval, but one must also check the parameter restrictions and possible boundary behavior. Categories sharing the same parameter factor can be combined in the score. Keep the chain-rule factor when differentiating log(1−θ/2), and distinguish likelihood maximization from simply equating every observed frequency with its model probability.''',[('A Bernoulli sample has 12 successes and 8 failures. Find the MLE of p.','Log likelihood is 12log p+8log(1−p). The score gives 12/p=8/(1−p), so p=12/20=.6; strict concavity confirms the maximum.'),('P(0)=θ/4,P(1)=1−θ/2,P(2)=θ/4, with counts 10,30,10. Find the interior MLE.','Likelihood is proportional to θ²⁰(1−θ/2)³⁰. Score 20/θ−15/(1−θ/2)=0 gives 20−10θ=15θ, so θ=.8.')])
add(54,'Statistical Inference',r'''One observation X has density f(x|θ)=3(θ−x)²/θ³ for 0<x<θ, zero otherwise. The interval (X/(1−λ₁),X/(1−λ₂)) has confidence coefficient 1−α, with 0<λ₁<λ₂<1. Which holds?''',[r'λ₂²−λ₁²=1−α.',r'λ₂³−λ₁³=1−α.',r'λ₂²−λ₁²=4(1−α).',r'λ₂³−λ₁³=9(1−α).'],'B',['The pivot W=1−X/θ has a parameter-free density.','Translate interval coverage into λ₁<W<λ₂.'],r'''Set V=X/θ; its density is 3(1−v)² for 0<v<1. Thus W=1−V has density 3w² on (0,1). The event X/(1−λ₁)<θ<X/(1−λ₂) is equivalent to 1−λ₂<X/θ<1−λ₁, hence λ₁<W<λ₂. Its probability is ∫_{λ₁}^{λ₂}3w²dw=λ₂³−λ₁³. Equating coverage to 1−α proves B.''',r'''A confidence interval is a random interval whose probability of covering the fixed unknown parameter equals a stated confidence coefficient under repeated sampling. A pivotal quantity is a function of the data and parameter with a distribution independent of the parameter; scale families often use X/θ. A one-to-one transformation of a continuous random variable changes its density by the absolute Jacobian of the inverse. Once a pivot's distribution is known, translate the coverage inequalities into bounds for that pivot and integrate its density. Care is needed when reciprocals or subtraction reverse inequalities. Strict versus non-strict endpoints do not affect coverage for continuous distributions. The coverage probability concerns the random data, whereas θ stays fixed; it is not a posterior probability assigned to θ.''',[('If X is uniform on (0,θ), find coverage of (X/b,X/a), 0<a<b<1.','The event is a<X/θ<b. Since X/θ is uniform on (0,1), coverage is b−a.'),('If W has density 2w on (0,1), find P(a<W<b).','Integrating gives ∫_a^b2w dw=b²−a². Thus that interval has target coverage 1−α when b²−a²=1−α.')])
add(55,'Statistical Inference',r'''X₁,…,Xₙ are iid with density θ2^θ/x^{θ+1} for x>2, θ>0. Define χ²_{m,β} by P(Y>χ²_{m,β})=β for Y~χ²_m. For testing H₀:θ=1 against H₁:θ>1, which is the size-α UMP rejection rule?''',[r'Σlog Xᵢ>(1/2)χ²_{2n,α}+nlog2.',r'Σlog Xᵢ<(1/2)χ²_{2n,1−α}+nlog2.',r'Σlog Xᵢ>χ²_{n,α}+nlog2.',r'Σlog Xᵢ<χ²_{n,1−α}+nlog2.'],'B',['Tᵢ=log(Xᵢ/2) has an exponential distribution with rate θ.','Large θ gives smaller sums, and under the null 2ΣTᵢ has χ²_{2n} distribution.'],r'''The survival P(X>2eᵗ)=e^{−θt} shows Tᵢ=log(Xᵢ/2)~Exp(rate θ). Their sum T=Σlog Xᵢ−nlog2 has likelihood θⁿe^{−θT}. For any θ>1 the likelihood ratio to θ=1 is θⁿe^{−(θ−1)T}, decreasing in T; the same lower-tail rejection set is most powerful for every alternative. Under H₀, 2T~χ²_{2n}. With the specified upper-tail quantile notation, P(2T<χ²_{2n,1−α})=α. Hence reject when Σlog Xᵢ<(1/2)χ²_{2n,1−α}+nlog2, option B.''',r'''A uniformly most powerful test maximizes power among all tests of a given size for every parameter in the specified alternative. The Neyman–Pearson lemma chooses large likelihood ratios for a simple alternative, and monotone likelihood ratios in a common statistic can yield the same rejection region across a one-sided family. Pareto-type scale observations become exponential variables under logarithmic transformation: log(X/a) has rate θ. A sum of n iid rate-one exponentials is gamma with shape n and scale one, and twice that sum is chi-square with 2n degrees of freedom. Increasing the exponential rate shifts observations downward, so the relevant rejection tail is lower. Quantile conventions must be read carefully: an upper-tail probability 1−α corresponds to a lower-tail probability α.''',[('Test Exp(rate θ) observations for θ=1 versus θ>1. State a size .05 rejection region for n observations.','Let T be their sum. Under the null 2T~χ²_{2n}; reject for 2T below the lower .05 quantile, equivalently below the upper-tail .95 quantile.'),('For one Exp(1) observation, find the lower-tail .1 cutoff.','P(T<c)=1−e^{−c}=.1 gives c=−log(.9). A test against a larger rate rejects when T<−log(.9).')])
add(56,'Statistical Inference',r'''X|θ~N(θ,15), and the improper prior on θ is proportional to 1 on ℝ. Observed X=13. Which statement is true?''',[r'Posterior mean=MLE of θ; posterior variance=Var(X|θ).',r'Posterior mean=MLE of θ; posterior variance<Var(X|θ).',r'Posterior mean>MLE of θ; posterior variance=Var(X|θ).',r'Posterior mean>MLE of θ; posterior variance<Var(X|θ).'],'A',['The flat prior leaves the likelihood kernel unchanged as a function of θ.','Normalize exp[−(θ−13)²/(2·15)] as a normal density in θ.'],r'''The likelihood as a function of θ is proportional to exp[−(13−θ)²/30]. Multiplication by the constant prior gives this same kernel, whose normalized posterior is N(13,15). Thus posterior mean is 13 and posterior variance is 15. The likelihood is maximized at θ=13, so the posterior mean equals the MLE, and posterior variance equals the conditional observation variance. A is correct.''',r'''Bayes' formula gives a posterior density proportional to likelihood times prior, provided the product has a finite positive normalizing integral. A constant prior on the whole real line is improper because it does not integrate to one, but it can yield a proper posterior. For a normal observation with known variance σ² and unknown mean θ, the likelihood in θ is a normal kernel centered at the observed value. A flat prior therefore produces N(x,σ²). With n independent observations it instead produces N(x̄,σ²/n). A proper normal prior adds precision and generally shrinks both the posterior mean and variance. Do not assume every Bayesian update reduces variance relative to the sampling variance; with one observation and a flat prior they are equal.''',[('Two iid N(θ,8) observations are 3 and 5, with flat prior. Find posterior.','The sample mean is 4. Completing the likelihood square gives posterior N(4,8/2)=N(4,4).'),('X|θ~N(θ,4) and θ~N(0,4); observe X=6. Find posterior.','Precisions add: posterior variance=(1/4+1/4)⁻¹=2. Mean=2(6/4+0/4)=3, so posterior is N(3,2).')])
add(57,'Regression and Multivariate Analysis',r'''For yᵢ=β₀+β₁x₁ᵢ+⋯+β₈x₈ᵢ+εᵢ, i=1,…,29, with iid N(0,σ²) errors, the least-squares F statistic for regression significance is 2.50. Find possible R² and adjusted R².''',['0.30 and 0.10','0.50 and 0.30','0.50 and 0.16','0.30 and −0.10'],'B',['There are p=8 slope terms and n−p−1=20 residual degrees of freedom.','Use F=(R²/p)/[(1−R²)/(n−p−1)].'],r'''F=(SSR/8)/(SSE/20)=(20/8)R²/(1−R²). Since F=2.5=20/8, R²/(1−R²)=1, giving R²=.5. Adjusted R²=1−(SSE/20)/(SST/28)=1−(1−.5)(28/20)=1−.7=.3. Thus option B is correct.''',r'''In ordinary least-squares regression with an intercept, total corrected sum of squares decomposes as SST=SSR+SSE. The coefficient of determination is R²=SSR/SST=1−SSE/SST. For n observations and p slope predictors, the global F statistic testing all slopes equal zero is (SSR/p)/(SSE/(n−p−1)), assuming a full-rank design matrix. Under independent normal errors with common variance, it has an F distribution under the null. Adjusted R² accounts for degrees of freedom: 1−[SSE/(n−p−1)]/[SST/(n−1)]. It can be negative and is generally lower than ordinary R². The intercept consumes one degree of freedom but is not counted among the p slopes in the global numerator. Use the same degrees of freedom consistently in both formulas.''',[('For n=21,p=4,R²=.4, compute F and adjusted R².','Residual degrees of freedom are 16. F=(.4/4)/(.6/16)=8/3. Adjusted R²=1−.6·20/16=.25.'),('If n=11,p=2 and F=4, find R².','F=(8/2)R²/(1−R²)=4R²/(1−R²). Thus R²=.5; adjusted R²=1−.5·10/8=.375.')])
add(58,'Regression and Multivariate Analysis',r'''(X₁,X₂,X₃)ᵀ~N₃(0,I₃), and (X,Y,Z)ᵀ=[[3,0,0],[2,2,0],[4,0,4]](X₁,X₂,X₃)ᵀ. Find the partial correlation ρ_{YZ·X}.''',[r'1/2',r'2/3',r'3/4','0'],'D',['Regress Y and Z on X=3X₁.','Their residuals involve independent coordinates X₂ and X₃.'],r'''X=3X₁, Y=2X₁+2X₂ and Z=4X₁+4X₃. The linear projection of Y onto X is (2/3)X and of Z onto X is (4/3)X. The residuals are 2X₂ and 4X₃, independent with positive variances. Their correlation is zero, so ρ_{YZ·X}=0. Equivalently covariance(Y,Z)=8 and covariance(Y,X)covariance(Z,X)/variance(X)=6·12/9=8, leaving zero partial covariance.''',r'''Partial correlation between Y and Z controlling for X is the correlation of their residuals after linear projection on X. For nondegenerate variables it equals (ρ_YZ−ρ_YXρ_ZX)/√[(1−ρ_YX²)(1−ρ_ZX²)]. The unstandardized residual covariance is Cov(Y,Z)−Cov(Y,X)Cov(Z,X)/Var(X). In a jointly normal model, zero partial correlation is equivalent to conditional independence given X, provided residual variances are positive. Independent standard normal coordinates make linear transformations easy to analyze: covariances are sums of products of matching coefficients. A common component can create marginal correlation that vanishes after controlling for the variable measuring that component. Determine residuals before normalizing; their independence or zero covariance may make a lengthy correlation calculation unnecessary.''',[('Let Y=A+B,Z=A+C,X=A with independent unit-variance A,B,C. Find partial correlation.','After projection on X the residuals are B,C. They have covariance zero and variances one, so partial correlation is zero, although marginal correlation is 1/2.'),('Let Y=A+B,Z=A+B+C,X=A with independent unit-variance components. Find partial correlation.','Residuals are B and B+C. Their covariance is 1 and variances are 1 and 2, giving ρ=1/√2.')])
add(59,'Sampling and Design of Experiments',r'''A population of size 85 contains 34 Statisticians and 51 Biologists. Independent SRSWOR samples draw 12 and 15 from those strata. Let ȳ_S,ȳ_B,ȳ denote the two stratum sample means and pooled sample mean. Which is an unbiased estimator of population mean Ȳ?''',[r'ȳ',r'(2ȳ_S+3ȳ_B)/5',r'(4ȳ_S+5ȳ_B)/9',r'(ȳ_S/12+ȳ_B/15)/(1/12+1/15)'],'B',['Each stratum sample mean is unbiased for its own population mean.','Combine them using population stratum proportions, not sample proportions.'],r'''Under SRSWOR, E(ȳ_S)=Ȳ_S and E(ȳ_B)=Ȳ_B. The target mean is (34/85)Ȳ_S+(51/85)Ȳ_B=(2/5)Ȳ_S+(3/5)Ȳ_B. Therefore (2ȳ_S+3ȳ_B)/5 is unbiased. The pooled sample uses weights 12/27=4/9 and 15/27=5/9, not the population weights; option C is precisely that pooled mean. Option D weights the strata 5/9 and 4/9, also incorrect in general.''',r'''Stratified sampling divides a finite population into disjoint strata and samples separately within each. Under simple random sampling without replacement, every subset of the required size in a stratum is equally likely and the stratum sample mean is unbiased for its population mean. The overall population mean is the weighted sum of stratum population means with weights W_h=N_h/N. Its unbiased estimator is therefore ΣW_hȳ_h, regardless of how sample sizes are allocated. A pooled unweighted sample mean instead uses n_h/n and is unbiased for the overall mean only under proportional allocation or special equal-mean populations. Without-replacement sampling affects the estimator's variance through finite-population corrections, but not these expectations. Distinguish population sizes from sample sizes when selecting weights; inverse sample-size weights do not generally estimate the desired mean.''',[('Two strata have population sizes 20,80 and sample means 10,30. Find the stratified estimate.','Weights are .2 and .8, so the estimate is .2·10+.8·30=26, irrespective of sample allocation.'),('Strata sizes are 60,40 and sample sizes 6,4. Is the pooled mean unbiased?','The sample proportions .6,.4 equal population proportions, so the pooled mean .6ȳ₁+.4ȳ₂ is the stratified unbiased estimator.')])
add(60,'Operations Research',r'''Maximize z=x+y subject to 5x+3y≤30, 2x+6y≤25, 2x−y≤8, x≥0,y≥0. Find the optimal objective value.''',[r'45/11',r'74/11',r'85/12',r'25/6'],'C',['Intersect the first two binding constraints and test the remaining constraint.','A nonnegative weighted sum of the first two inequalities gives a matching objective upper bound.'],r'''Solving 5x+3y=30 and 2x+6y=25 gives x=35/8,y=65/24. Both are positive and 2x−y=145/24≤8, so the point is feasible. Its objective is 35/8+65/24=170/24=85/12. To certify optimality, multiply the first inequality by 1/6 and the second by 1/12. The left side becomes x+y and the right side becomes 30/6+25/12=85/12. Every feasible point has z≤85/12, and the constructed point attains this bound.''',r'''A linear program optimizes a linear objective over a polyhedron defined by linear inequalities. In two variables the feasible region is an intersection of half-planes, and a finite optimum is attained at a boundary vertex or along an optimal edge when the feasible set is nonempty and bounded in the objective direction. A candidate obtained by intersecting two constraint boundaries must satisfy all remaining inequalities, including nonnegativity. Optimality can be certified without listing every vertex: a nonnegative linear combination of constraint inequalities that equals the objective provides an upper bound for maximization. A feasible point attaining that bound is optimal by weak duality. The multipliers are dual variables, and unused constraints may have multiplier zero. Never accept an intersection solely because it yields a large objective; feasibility is an independent requirement.''',[('Maximize x+y subject to x+2y≤6,2x+y≤6,x,y≥0.','Adding the two inequalities gives 3(x+y)≤12, hence z≤4. The feasible intersection x=y=2 attains 4, certifying optimality.'),('Maximize 2x+y subject to x+y≤3,x≤2,x,y≥0.','Add x+y≤3 to x≤2 to obtain 2x+y≤5. The feasible point (2,1) attains 5, so the maximum is 5.')],track='Mathematics')
print(w.path,len(w.packs))
